# /init -- write this repository's AGENTS.md

Produce the AGENTS.md that every future session in this repository starts with.
It is loaded automatically from the git root down to the working directory and
prepended to every conversation, so its length is a permanent cost: make it earn it.

## 1. Find what already exists -- never overwrite blindly
Check the repository root and the working directory for an instruction file the
loader already reads: AGENTS.md, AGENT.md, CLAUDE.md, or .claude/CLAUDE.md. Also
look for rules other tools left behind: .grok/rules/, .claude/rules/, .cursor/rules/,
.github/copilot-instructions.md, CONTRIBUTING.md.

If one of those instruction files exists, READ IT FIRST and treat it as the source of
truth. Improve it in place: correct what is wrong, add what is missing, delete what
the code no longer does. Do not regenerate it from scratch, and do not drop a rule you
cannot show is obsolete -- someone put it there for a reason you may not have hit yet.
If no instruction file exists, create AGENTS.md at the repository root. Fold anything
useful from the other tools' rule files into it rather than leaving it stranded.

## 2. Investigate before you write
Every line you write must come from a file you actually read, not from how projects
like this usually work. At minimum read the README and CONTRIBUTING, the build and
package manifests, the CI workflow (usually the most reliable source for the real
build/test/lint commands), the linter and formatter configs, and enough of the source
tree to describe the architecture honestly. Prefer confirming a command exists over
asserting it does.

## 3. What the file must cover
In this order, skipping any heading that genuinely does not apply:

- Overview -- one or two sentences: what this project is and what it produces.
- Commands -- the exact build, test, lint, format, and run invocations,
  copy-pasteable. Include how to run a SINGLE test or one package's tests; that is
  usually the most valuable line in the file. Note any environment variable, toolchain
  version manager, or setup step that is easy to miss and expensive to get wrong.
- Architecture -- the things that take an hour to work out from the source: what the
  top-level modules/crates/packages own, where the entry points are, how a request or
  build flows through them, and which directories are generated or vendored and must
  never be hand-edited.
- Conventions -- this project's own style where it departs from the language default:
  naming, error handling, logging, test layout, import ordering. Only what a reviewer
  would actually reject a patch over.
- Gotchas -- the rules that cause real breakage: pre-commit hooks, generated files,
  migration ordering, platform-specific steps, tests that need a flag or a long
  timeout. State the commit-message or PR convention if the repo has one.

## 4. Keep it earned
- Aim for roughly 30-60 lines. Density beats coverage.
- Write instructions, not description: "run `<test command> <one test>`", not "this
  project has tests".
- Cut anything an agent finds faster by looking: file listings, dependency dumps,
  paraphrases of the README.
- Cut transient state: open bugs, in-flight work, TODOs. It goes stale silently.
- Do not invent a convention the code does not follow. An unverified rule is worse
  than a missing one.
- In a monorepo keep the root file general and put package-specific rules in that
  package's own AGENTS.md -- deeper files are loaded too and win on conflict.

## 5. Finish
Write the file, then report its path and a short summary of what went in it. If you
improved an existing file, say exactly what you changed and why.
